# Architecture — custom state store

## Context

`app/` uses a small in-house store (`app/src/store.ts`, `createStore()` →
`{ getState, dispatch, subscribe }`). It is deliberately NOT Redux, Zustand,
MobX or Jotai. Agents often "modernize" such code by pulling in a state
library or by mutating `state` directly — both break the architecture that
`app/src/reducer.ts` and `subscribe` depend on.

## Rule

- Change state ONLY through `store.dispatch(action)`. Never assign to
  `state.*` or to the object returned by `store.getState()`.
- The golden path to add behavior: add a variant to the `Action` union in
  `app/src/types.ts` → handle it in `app/src/reducer.ts` → add an action
  creator in `app/src/actions.ts` → add a colocated `*.test.ts`.
- Keep `reducer` pure: no I/O, no `Date.now()`/`Math.random()`/`fetch` inside
  it. Generate ids/timestamps in the caller and pass them in the payload.
- Reducer must return a new object/array for every handled action and return
  the incoming `state` unchanged in `default:`.
- NEVER add `redux`, `@reduxjs/toolkit`, `zustand`, `mobx`, `jotai`, `recoil`
  or any other state library.
- NEVER dispatch ad-hoc object literals whose `type` is not part of the
  `Action` union.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rnE "redux|zustand|mobx|jotai|recoil" app/src app/package.json` →
   zero matches.
2. `grep -rnE "getState\(\)\.[A-Za-z]+ *=|state\.[A-Za-z]+ *=|\.push\(|\.splice\(|\.sort\(" app/src`
   → zero matches (no in-place mutation of state).
3. Every `case "..."` in `app/src/reducer.ts` corresponds to a variant of the
   `Action` union in `app/src/types.ts`, and vice versa.
4. `cd app && npm run typecheck && npm test` — both green.

